uh uh that's what i have to say thank you for your time eric thank you mr mckinney anyone else like to speak thank you mr mayor city's cities members of the city council i appreciate you having given us so much time on this issue can you identify yourself i'm sorry please i'm sorry my name is john rivera i'm also a member of the board of directors of the wyoming promise i've been working on this issue for a number of years and i approached a couple of city council members last fall to discuss the possibility of having a resolution like this considered and i particularly would like to express my appreciation to mr cook and mr layburn who were interested in it and

willing to go forward but the timing was bad because it was getting close to an election and there were other issues that kind of stymied us but then also dr aldrich has been a great help she is actually the sponsor of the legislation and unfortunately i probably didn't give her enough information about uh mr cook and mr laborn's involvement before then and that's that's my fault that they probably aren't as prominent as they should be on the issue but i thank all members of the city council because you've lent your ear to us you've heard the issues and we're here hoping that you will carry this matter forward as a matter of background for those who we haven't spoken to wyman promise is a cross-partisan organization it was originally started by a number of people including senator allen simpson who was a primary mover of creating the board of or not the board but the organization uh

wyoming promise and we went through a petition drive and i as i said at the finance committee meeting we had collected over 25 000 signatures but in doing our own review of those 25 signatures we tossed 5 000 of them because full for a number of reasons one some signatures were illegible some people who thought they were registered to vote were ineligible to sign the petition because they hadn't voted in the last general election and then there was a situation that some people had voted twice uh signed the petition twice on where the forgotten that they'd uh signed it at a previous year but uh nevertheless we never submitted the uh the petition with the signatures to the secretary of state because we knew that we weren't going to meet the requirements to get it on the ballot part of that reason too is also because wyoming has the most onerous petition requirements in the surrounding states had the percentages been presented in any other state percentage of the signatures been presented in any of the surrounding states our petition would have been on the ballot but

as a as we all know it wasn't so wyoming promises focus on the legislature to try to get the legislature to pass a resolution to congress to comply with article 5 of the united states constitution and thus far 22 states that we're aware of have signed such a petition and if the state of wyoming would have considered the issue this year and successfully passed a resolution we would have become the 23rd state 34 states are required to require to get congress to actually uh propose an amendment to the constitution u.s constitution our bill this year uh didn't even get introduced uh because the session was was conflicted a lot by by covid it was trifurcated there was the first couple weeks that there was a meeting of committees mr rivera if i could get you to speak to the resolution before so

i do appreciate all this information but we do have a resolution in front of us and i'd like to have your comments specifically to that please if you would thank you thank you specifically this is a request to have the city of cheyenne support a resolution to the constitution now it would have no legal effect because the only ones that the congress is required to consider are resolutions submitted by states i think a resolution by a city is very important because congress can on its own propose resolutions and if enough communities in the nation were to express their opinion in support of such a resolution then congress may listen on their own and even if even if congress doesn't perhaps the state legislature will hear wyoming citizens and

theirs and the cities in the states in the state uh expressing the support of such a resolution that they may pass it next time that it's presented to the legislature currently there are 836 cities nationwide that have approved a resolution and we would really love to see the city of cheyenne become the leader in the state to show that they support the resolution also you could be the shining light for the rest of the cities in the state and in fact we'll probably be talking with wham wyman association municipalities the county commissioners association and there are other cities actually considering this i believe i don't know they've gotten it as far as you have as a city council but the cities of laramie and

cities a city of lander have already been discussing this although nothing formal has been presented to the city councils but we're requesting that the city council cheyenne become the shining light in the state we'll provide the leadership to the other cities in the state to get their support for a resolution to amend the u.s constitution thank you mr revere thank you and i stand for any questions if there are any we appreciate you thank you anyone else like to speak to this item mayor collins and council members i'm albert simpson and i live here in cheyenne and i'd just like to make a note that last year i saw a note that there'd be meeting at the library for this program and

