specifically to uh ask congress to take up a and take up a constitutional amendment that would empower it to curtail political speech um if there's one thing that we know about uh the political election process uh over time it's that uh just me standing on a on a city uh intersection corner has or even in a public park has lost lost its power and it's it's critical that uh people organizations companies that have issues that are important to them be able to spend money to to speak out one of the other things that we've seen is we talk about a canceled culture now and the idea that if if a company speaks up on an issue it's subject to backlash and

and so sometimes it's important uh for companies organizations to band together to be able to spend money to speak out on public issues and and to speak freely and and therefore i am deeply concerned with this what appears to be an effort to erode one of the the purest bastions of our freedom and that's our ability to speak freely on issues of public policy and political concern whether it's whether it's our coal companies in in the powder river basin or or maybe a movie studio that's a corporation so i'd urge you to vote against the resolution thank you thank you mr wiggum michael any other comments online no not seeing anything else at this time there thank

you is there anyone else in the audience like to speak to the resolution hearing none mr roybal mr mayor the recommendation of the finance committee is to adopt an iso move second it's been moved by mr roybal seconded by mr layborn comments from members of the governing mr mayor mr labor well this certainly is unusual for the council to take such a matter under consideration but i think we all understand and look back over what's happened to our political process since there was the determination by the supreme court that corporations were people and could contribute funds to political campaigns just as we can and just as we do here in the city of cheyenne and

i would ask each member of the council if they haven't noticed the deterioration of our political process on a national and statewide level when these races can be flooded by untraceable money the suggestion that this is a constriction of free speech is very strange to me this is a regulation that we've had for years that has made sense in our campaigns where we seek contributions from individuals and notify the clerk's office in fact we have the consideration over the past a few years to have that released a little bit early so people can evaluate it as they make their choices but i think it's really important to note that this money is untraceable whether it is coming from the united states of america where of course we are under the first amendment to the constitution or from who knows where for who knows what purpose and

the suggestion that it is something far away from us certainly was not borne out in the election the last election where some of us were elected and filed our campaign finance report in the state of wyoming there were a number of races and a number of challenges to this process where large amounts of money were dumped into those elections in the last few weeks and that is a very dangerous thing for us here on the city council and i fully recognize that this is something that probably won't make a huge difference but the difference is that we're speaking up and we're saying that our elections matter and that we want to see accountability in those contributions because what we've seen from the federal level now to the state level is inevitably without more control and

understanding going to come down to our level and so i'd ask each of us how we'd feel if a and these generally are very negative campaigns i might add the last couple weeks of one of our campaigns all of a sudden this unaccountable money goes into an advertising campaign that we can't answer and that has happened it's happened in the state of wyoming so i think that this is justified in saying that we want to join the other municipalities and the other states in declaring that corporations are not people and they do not have free speech rights under the first amendment uh i would point out that this country for more than 200 and i don't know how many years had didn't have that provision we operated under the idea that a citizen and

individual had that right but a corporation did not and i think we've seen the power of corporations to and these are corporations that have are internationally owned enter into this process so i appreciate the consideration that we've gotten on this issue and i would point out that this is probably an opportunity for us to be a leader in the state of wyoming and to state our position here which i think is appropriate thank you thank you mr labor anyone else on the governing body like to speak mayor collins dr aldridge um i am a firm supporter of the first amendment but i also am a firm supporter of accountability and i think that when we're talking about our electoral process which is sacred and

something that needs to be um taken care of that it's important that there be accountability and that the dollars be traceable and um for that reason i was willing to sponsor this bill with my fellow councilman mr layborn and also mr cook who had sponsored it last year or worked to support it and i think that it's important this is a nonpartisan issue it affects both parties and i think that this is something that we need to support and we need to as a capital city of our state need to take a stand and be a leader mr escobel thank you mr mayor through you and i've actually been looking at this issue for 20 years and

when i first wanted to do some research on it i read a book it was printed in the 1960s called the imperial congress crisis and the separation of powers and it was written by the claremont institute and the heritage foundation and in that book it talked about a fourth branch of government and the emergence of that branch of government being the bureaucracy of money and how it was going to have influence over the other three branches of government and how it was going to crumble our democracy well the founding fathers one of the things they put into our democracy that's made it live this long was a strong system of checks and balances and having that separation of powers has been something that's been a cornerstone of our democracy and

people think that it's not here or it's not close right now on channel five there is an ad where it's pitting one laramie county senator against another not only that we had a representative from the everglades state come to our state capitol and speak against our state elected official on a vote she made of conscience and dark money was involved in both of those things that's why for one i am a strong supporter of it but now we're starting to get to the point where elected officials can pick their voters now we're getting to the point where they can pick their opponents that's just wrong thank you mr esquivel anyone else from the governing body like to speak mr johnson um

